Jul 29 22:45:51.767 DBG agent/conversation.go:474 agent.tool_call tool=edit_file id=call_f5c2dae143a14791b3250344 args="map[new_string:- Invalid on `get` steps (a get fans the remainder of the plan out per version, so a conditional get has no coherent meaning).\n- This is how an agent decides routing without typed outputs: an `agent` step writes a verdict into a declared output artifact, and the next step's `when:` tests that file. The model proposes; a deterministic command disposes; both are visible in the YAML.\n\n## Tolerated failure (`try:`)\n\nA `task`/`put`/`agent` step can be wrapped with `try:` to make its failure tolerable — the plan continues past it as if it succeeded, while the real failure is still recorded, its hooks still fire, and its `to:` routing still sees the truth. This is the same `scalar ⸢ mapping` YAML idiom as `when:`/`handoff:`/`fix:`.\n\n```yaml\n- try:\n    task: notify-slack\n    run: ./notify.sh\n    on_failure:\n      put: status\n  # plan continues regardless of notify-slack's outcome\n- task: build\n  run: ./build.sh\n```\n\n- **Purpose**: best-effort work like notifications, metrics pushes, cleanup steps that shouldn't block the pipeline.\n- **What happens**: the inner step's real outcome is recorded, its hooks fire normally, and `to:` routes on the truth — but the plan continues if the step fails.\n- **Log output**: `try: <name> (failed, continuing)` on failure, `try: <name> (succeeded)` on success.\n- **Composability**: nests with `attempts:`/`timeout:` on the inner step (retries happen before the try swallows the final failure), with `when:` on the inner step (the guard fires first), with `fix:` on a task (the fix agent runs, then the re-run outcome is what the try sees), and with `assert:` on the inner step (assert is evaluated against the real output; a matching assert clears the failure so the try sees success).\n- **Test-mode**: `assert.execution` records the inner step's name (not a synthetic name), and `assert:` on the inner step checks the real outcome.\n- **Invalid on `get` steps** (a get fans the remainder of the plan out per version, so wrapping one is meaningless) and on another `try:` (nesting has no useful semantics).\n- **Valid inside a hook**: a hook step can be `try:`-wrapped so the hook's failure doesn't fail the enclosing step.\n- **Merkle**: a `try:`-wrapped step's chain is always unskippable (the inner step's real failure must be re-executed on rerun so its hooks fire and its assert is re-checked). The `try:` wrapper itself is folded into the step's content hash, so adding/removing `try:` busts the cache.\n\n## Step transitions (`to:`/`max_visits:`/`verdicts:`) old_string:- Invalid on `get` steps (a get fans the remainder of the plan out per version, so a conditional get has no coherent meaning).\n- This is how an agent decides routing without typed outputs: an `agent` step writes a verdict into a declared output artifact, and the next step's `when:` tests that file. The model proposes; a deterministic command disposes; both are visible in the YAML.\n\n## Step transitions (`to:`/`max_visits:`/`verdicts:`) path:repo/docs/control-flow.md]"
